
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Network Topologies</title>
<style>
body{font-family:Arial,sans-serif;background:#effaf4;color:#204637;margin:0;padding:25px}
header{text-align:center;background:#218c74;color:white;padding:25px;border-radius:18px}
main{max-width:850px;margin:25px auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:16px}
article{background:white;padding:20px;border-radius:15px;box-shadow:0 4px 10px #d5e7dc}
.diagram{font-family:monospace;font-size:19px;white-space:pre-line;text-align:center;background:#e4f6ec;padding:18px;border-radius:10px}
h2{color:#218c74}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<header><h1>🌐 Network Topologies</h1><p>How computers can be arranged in a network.</p></header>
<main>
<article><h2>Star Topology</h2><div class="diagram"> PC ─┐
PC ─┼─ Switch
PC ─┘</div><p>Devices connect to a central switch or hub. Easy to manage, but the central device matters.</p></article>
<article><h2>Bus Topology</h2><div class="diagram">PC ─ PC ─ PC ─ PC</div><p>Devices share one main communication cable.</p></article>
<article><h2>Ring Topology</h2><div class="diagram">PC ─ PC
│　　 │
PC ─ PC</div><p>Each device connects to neighboring devices in a ring.</p></article>
<article><h2>Mesh Topology</h2><div class="diagram">A ─── B
|\　 /|
| \ / |
| / \ |
C ─── D</div><p>Devices have multiple connections, which can provide alternate paths.</p></article>
<article><h2>Tree Topology</h2><div class="diagram">　Root
　 /　\
 A　　B
/ \　 / \
C D　E F</div><p>Devices are organized in a hierarchy of branches.</p></article>
<article><h2>Hybrid Topology</h2><div class="diagram">Star + Bus
or
Star + Ring</div><p>Combines two or more network topology types.</p></article>
</main>
</body>
</html>
